      "study_summary": {
        "title": "Framing the Two Halves: A Spatial Study of Syndromes and a Century",
        "central_argument": "A filmmaker can test whether framing, camera position, and spatial repetition distinguish the two halves of Syndromes and a Century by treating the film as a controlled experiment in visual grammar. The critic's report of a shift from warm, intimate rural scenes to cold, detached urban ones (C2) suggests that the film's spatial logic may change between halves, but this claim must be verified through close viewing. Using Bordwell and Thompson's concept of constructive editing (S3), we can ask whether the first half builds space through fragments that imply a coherent offscreen world, while the second half uses more complete or more disorienting framings. Katz's discussion of camera position and focal length (S8) provides a method for logging how the same subject is framed differently across halves. The central argument is that the film's two-part structure invites a comparative analysis of spatial relations, and that any observed differences in framing, camera movement, or repetition are hypotheses to be tested against the film, not facts to be asserted from memory.",
        "section_lenses": [
          "Constructive editing and offscreen space",
          "Camera position and focal length",
          "Spatial repetition and the square fitness exercise",
          "Screen direction and the 180-degree system",
          "Deep focus and spatial layering"
        ],
