      "study_summary": {
        "title": "Framing Desire: Constraint, Offscreen Space, and Repetition in In the Mood for Love",
        "central_argument": "In the Mood for Love builds its emotional architecture less through what it shows than through what it withholds. The film's constrained framing, its systematic use of offscreen space, and its patterned repetitions do not simply decorate a story of unconsummated love; they constitute its primary expressive logic. By treating the frame as a boundary that both hides and implies, Wong Kar-wai turns the viewer into an active participant in the characters' restraint. This study proposes that the film's formal strategies—narrow doorways, obstructed views, repeated slow-motion passages, and the conspicuous absence of the adulterous spouses—create a viewing experience in which desire is felt as pressure against limits. Because no clip evidence is supplied, every formal claim below is a viewing hypothesis to be tested against the film itself, and every critical interpretation is treated as a report to be verified, not as proof of intention.",
        "section_lenses": [
          "Constrained framing and the doorway motif",
          "Offscreen space and the absent spouses",
          "Repetition and the slow-motion passages",
          "Constructive editing and spatial fragmentation",
          "Repetition of visual motifs and symbolic restraint"
